"""New Chapter 3 text blocks for the revised paper (past tense, describes what was done)."""

DESIGN = """<h2 id="research-design">Research Design</h2>
<p>This study employed a developmental research design with a quantitative approach to develop and evaluate AgriKA-GIS, a Web-based spatial decision-support system for barangay-level rice productivity monitoring in Laguna. Developmental research was appropriate because the study involved the design, development, enhancement, and evaluation of both a predictive model and a software system. As described by Richey and Klein (2007), developmental research focuses on the systematic design, development, and evaluation of products and tools intended to address practical problems. In this study, the products were the enhanced AgriKA CNN-LSTM rice yield prediction model, the AgriKA-GIS platform, and the automated data pipeline that supplies both.</p>
<p>A quantitative approach was used because the study relied on numerical data: historical rice yield records, satellite remote sensing observations, meteorological variables, model prediction outputs, pipeline run records, and user evaluation scores. Quantitative methods allowed the researchers to assess objectively the predictive performance of the enhanced model, the reliability and security of the pipeline, and the acceptability of the system to its intended users.</p>
<p>The study consisted of three development and evaluation components that correspond to the three specific objectives. The first was the extension of the existing CNN-LSTM model with Sentinel-1 SAR backscatter, evaluated with the Root Mean Square Error (RMSE), the Mean Absolute Error (MAE), and the Coefficient of Determination (R²) against an equivalent model without Sentinel-1. The second was the Web-GIS, which presents observed and predicted yields and their residuals at municipality and barangay level under role-based access, evaluated through functional and compatibility testing and through User Acceptance Testing (UAT) based on the ISO/IEC 25010 software quality model. The third was the data pipeline, evaluated on its reliability, its unattended operation on a weekly schedule, and a set of security test cases.</p>
<p>The UAT covered the six ISO/IEC 25010 characteristics judged most relevant to the system: functional suitability, performance efficiency, usability, reliability, security, and maintainability. These characteristics assess whether the system performs its intended functions correctly, responds efficiently, is easy for its intended users to operate, remains dependable, protects agricultural data, and can be maintained as monitoring requirements change.</p>
"""

LOCALE_ADD = """<p>The User Acceptance Testing was later extended to agricultural personnel of the Municipal Agriculture Offices of Victoria, Nagcarlan, and Calauan, as described under the population of the study.</p>"""

POPULATION = """<h2>Population of the Study</h2>
<p>The population of this study consisted of the agricultural personnel directly involved in rice production monitoring, agricultural planning, field validation, reporting, and data management in the Local Government Unit agriculture offices of Laguna and in the Provincial Agriculture Office. These officers are the intended end users of AgriKA-GIS and served as the respondents for its evaluation.</p>
<p>At the start of the study, the accessible population was identified from institutional data and stakeholder consultations as 26 agricultural personnel: 24 city-level officers and technicians of the four target cities (one Agricultural Technologist from Biñan; one City Agriculturist, one Rice Manager, one Coordinator, and eleven designated area technicians from Cabuyao; one City Agriculturist, one Supervising Agriculturist, two Senior Agriculturists, three rice technicians, and one Rice Report Officer from Calamba; and one City Agriculturist from Santa Rosa), together with the Provincial Agriculturist and the Provincial Rice Report Officer of the Provincial Agriculture Office in Santa Cruz.</p>
<p>The evaluation was later opened to agricultural personnel of other Local Government Unit agriculture offices in Laguna. The final respondents were 19 agricultural personnel from eight offices: the City Agriculture Offices of Biñan (1), Santa Rosa (4), Calamba (3), and Cabuyao (1); the Municipal Agriculture Offices of Victoria (1), Nagcarlan (3), and Calauan (1); and the Provincial Agriculture Office in Santa Cruz (5).</p>
<p>Local rice farmers were not respondents in the system evaluation. Their reported seasonal harvests instead served as the ground-level source of the recorded yield data used as ground truth for training and validating the prediction model. The Municipality of Los Baños likewise participated only during the requirements-gathering stage, providing operational and technical insights that informed the system design rather than serving as an evaluation site.</p>
<h2>Sampling Techniques</h2>
<p>The study used purposive sampling. Every member of the accessible population identified at the outset was invited to take part (total enumeration), and the invitation was later extended to agricultural personnel of other Laguna agriculture offices, as described above. Respondents were included on the basis of their direct involvement in rice production monitoring, field validation, agricultural reporting, or agricultural planning, which makes them appropriate evaluators of AgriKA-GIS as its intended end users. Nineteen personnel completed the evaluation.</p>
<p>A purposively selected group of informed users is appropriate for developmental research, where the objective is to obtain informed evaluations from individuals with relevant domain expertise rather than to generalize findings to a larger population (Richey &amp; Klein, 2007; Etikan et al., 2016). This approach is consistent with the previous AgriKA study, which evaluated its system using a small panel of agricultural experts and obtained a Cronbach's Alpha of 0.8442, indicating good internal consistency (Ebron et al., 2025). Similar LGU-based agricultural decision-support studies have likewise adopted purposive sampling, recognizing that the expertise and operational experience of evaluators are more important than sample size in assessing system usability and practical applicability (Panda et al., 2025; Odion et al., 2023).</p>
"""

TOOLS_AND_PROCEDURE = """<h3 id="data-gathering-tools">Data Gathering Tools</h3>
<p>The study used both primary and secondary data. Primary data were collected through stakeholder interviews and User Acceptance Testing, while secondary data consisted of historical rice production records, satellite remote sensing observations, meteorological records, and administrative boundary datasets. The previous AgriKA study (Ebron et al., 2025) served as a documentary reference for the existing CNN-LSTM model, including its architecture, feature engineering, training method, and evaluation, so that the enhanced model could be compared with it on consistent terms.</p>
<h3 id="structured-stakeholder-interviews">Structured Stakeholder Interviews</h3>
<p>Structured interviews were conducted with City Agriculturists, Rice Technicians, and other personnel of the City Agriculture Offices of Biñan, Cabuyao, Calamba, Santa Rosa, and Los Baños, and with the Provincial Agriculture Office in Santa Cruz. The interview guide consisted of predetermined open-ended questions on existing rice production monitoring practices, reporting workflows, data management procedures, and the challenges encountered in routine monitoring.</p>
<p>The interviews identified the functional and non-functional requirements of AgriKA-GIS, including the information needs of agricultural personnel, preferred visualization methods, reporting requirements, and desired system functions. According to Hove and Anda (2005), structured interviews provide consistent and reliable information while allowing practitioners to share operational knowledge that cannot be obtained from documentary sources alone. The responses served as the basis for the system requirements and were revisited when the system was demonstrated to City Agriculture Office personnel at the end of the first development iteration.</p>
<h3 id="remote-sensing-data">Remote Sensing Data</h3>
<p>Satellite observations served as the primary environmental data source for rice yield prediction. The study used Sentinel-2 multispectral imagery and Sentinel-1 SAR imagery from the European Space Agency's Copernicus Programme, accessed through the Sentinel Hub services of the Copernicus Data Space Ecosystem, the same services used by the previous AgriKA study (Ebron et al., 2025).</p>
<p>From Sentinel-2 Level-2A surface reflectance, three vegetation indices were computed: the Normalized Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI), which contrasts near-infrared and red reflectance to describe vegetation vigor; the Enhanced Vegetation Index (EVI), which reduces atmospheric and soil-background effects and is less prone to saturation in dense canopy; and the Normalized Difference Water Index (NDWI), computed from the green and near-infrared bands to describe surface water and canopy moisture. These indices are widely used for crop monitoring and rice yield prediction (Fernandez-Beltran et al., 2021; Saini &amp; Nagpal, 2024).</p>
<p>From Sentinel-1 Interferometric Wide swath Ground Range Detected products, the VV and VH backscatter were obtained as terrain-corrected gamma-nought values converted to decibels. Because radar penetrates cloud, Sentinel-1 provides observations of crop structure and surface moisture in months when the optical imagery is obscured, particularly in the wet season (Wu et al., 2020).</p>
<h3 id="meteorological-data">Meteorological Data</h3>
<p>Meteorological variables were obtained from the Open-Meteo API: rainfall, air temperature, and relative humidity. These are the same source and the same three variables used by the previous AgriKA study (Ebron et al., 2025), which keeps the meteorological inputs of the two models comparable. Values were aggregated per month for each area, as total rainfall and as mean temperature and mean relative humidity, so that they align with the monthly satellite observations.</p>
<h3 id="historical-rice-yield-records">Historical Rice Yield Records</h3>
<p>Historical rice production records served as the ground truth for model training and evaluation. Municipality-level seasonal yields in metric tons per hectare were compiled from the Philippine Rice Information System (PRiSM) records maintained by PhilRice for the rice-producing cities and municipalities of Laguna, covering the dry and wet seasons from 2018 to 2025.</p>
<p>Barangay-level harvest records were obtained from the City Agriculture Offices of Biñan, Cabuyao, Calamba, and Santa Rosa, covering 42 rice-producing barangays. These records were used to evaluate predictions at barangay level and to compute the barangay residual maps.</p>
<h3 id="geospatial-data">Geospatial Data</h3>
<p>Municipality and barangay boundaries for the Province of Laguna were obtained from publicly available administrative boundary datasets of the Philippine Statistics Authority. The boundaries define the areas over which the satellite and weather values were summarized and provide the spatial framework of the AgriKA-GIS maps, where predicted yields and residuals are linked to their municipality or barangay polygons.</p>
<h3 id="user-acceptance-testing-uat-questionnaire">User Acceptance Testing (UAT) Questionnaire</h3>
<p>The acceptability of AgriKA-GIS was evaluated with a 14-item structured questionnaire based on the ISO/IEC 25010 software quality model, covering six characteristics: functional suitability, performance efficiency, usability, reliability, security, and maintainability. Each statement was rated on a four-point Likert scale from Strongly Disagree (1) to Strongly Agree (4). The instrument was adapted from the previous AgriKA study (Ebron et al., 2025) to keep the evaluation of the two systems consistent, and it was administered as an online form.</p>
<h3 id="data-gathering-procedure">Data Gathering Procedure</h3>
<p>Data were gathered in five phases.</p>
<p><b>Phase 1. Stakeholder Coordination and Requirements Gathering.</b> The researchers coordinated with the participating City Agriculture Offices and the Provincial Agriculture Office and conducted the structured interviews between May and June 2026, documenting existing monitoring workflows, reporting procedures, operational challenges, and user requirements.</p>
<p><b>Phase 2. Collection of Secondary Datasets.</b> Municipality-level yield records were compiled from PhilRice and barangay-level harvest records were requested from the four City Agriculture Offices. Satellite and meteorological data were then acquired by the data pipeline for the 30 cities and municipalities of Laguna and for the 42 barangays with reported yields, from January 2018 onward.</p>
<p><b>Phase 3. Data Preparation.</b> The satellite and weather data were prepared as monthly values for each area, as follows.</p>
<h3>Monthly Feature Extraction</h3>
<p>Rather than selecting individual images, the pipeline requested monthly statistics for each municipality and barangay boundary from the Sentinel Hub Statistical API. This produces one consistent value per area per month for every source and can be repeated automatically each week, which the third objective requires.</p>
<p>1. Sentinel-2. For each month and boundary, the Statistical API composed the Level-2A acquisitions of that month into a monthly mosaic at a 20-meter sampling resolution, after excluding scenes above a set cloud-cover threshold. Pixels classified by the scene classification layer as cloud shadow, medium- or high-probability cloud, thin cirrus, or snow were excluded, and the NDVI, EVI, and NDWI were computed for the remaining pixels. The monthly mean and standard deviation of each index within the boundary were recorded, together with the number of valid pixels. A month with no valid pixel was recorded as missing.</p>
<p>2. Sentinel-1. For each month and boundary, the VV and VH backscatter of the monthly Sentinel-1 mosaic were terrain-corrected, converted to decibels, and averaged within the boundary. Pixels without a valid positive backscatter value were excluded.</p>
<p>3. Weather. Daily Open-Meteo values were aggregated to monthly total rainfall, mean temperature, and mean relative humidity for each area.</p>
<p>4. Storage. The monthly values were loaded into the feature tables of the PostgreSQL/PostGIS database, keyed by area, year, and month, and checked against their physical ranges.</p>
"""

MODEL = """<h3>Enhanced CNN-LSTM Model Development</h3>
<p>The previous AgriKA CNN-LSTM model (Ebron et al., 2025) was reimplemented in PyTorch from the architecture and settings reported for its final configuration, and the same architecture was trained twice: a baseline configuration with the input classes available to the previous model (rainfall, temperature, relative humidity, and Sentinel-2 NDVI), and an enhanced configuration that added the Sentinel-1 VV and VH backscatter. Keeping the architecture, target, training procedure, and observations identical isolates the contribution of Sentinel-1. Table 3 summarizes the configuration.</p>
<h3 id="table-3">Table 3</h3><h3>CNN-LSTM Model Training Configuration</h3>
<table><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Configuration</th></tr>
<tr><td>Input</td><td>Six monthly timesteps per municipality-season. Baseline: rainfall, temperature, relative humidity, NDVI. Enhanced: the same plus Sentinel-1 VV and VH backscatter.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Target</td><td>Observed seasonal rice yield of the municipality (metric tons per hectare).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Architecture</td><td>Gaussian noise (0.01); Conv1D (64 filters, kernel 5) and Conv1D (128 filters, kernel 5), each with batch normalization and ReLU; LSTM (128 units) and LSTM (64 units), each with layer normalization and dropout of 0.3; Dense (64) and Dense (32) with ReLU and dropout of 0.3; Dense output (1 unit, linear). Pooling omitted (see below).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Optimizer</td><td>Adam, initial learning rate 4.5 × 10⁻⁴, reduced by a factor of 0.9 every 25 epochs; L2 regularization (weight decay) 1 × 10⁻⁴.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Loss function</td><td>Mean Squared Error (MSE).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Batch size and epochs</td><td>Batch size 16; at most 500 epochs.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Early stopping</td><td>Training stopped when the validation loss had not improved for 60 consecutive epochs; the weights with the lowest validation loss were retained.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Validation</td><td>Leave-one-year-out cross-validation over the eight years from 2018 to 2025; within each fold, 15% of the training records were held out for early stopping.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Evaluation metrics</td><td>RMSE, MAE, and R² over the pooled out-of-fold predictions.</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Two adaptations of the previous architecture were necessary. Pooling was omitted because the input sequences consist of six monthly timesteps, and pooling would have collapsed the time axis that the LSTM layers are meant to model. Recurrent dropout was approximated with dropout on the LSTM outputs, because the accelerated LSTM implementation in PyTorch does not support it. Both adaptations apply equally to the two configurations.</p>
<h4>Data Dictionary</h4>
<p>Table 4 lists the candidate input attributes and the target variable. The candidate predictors were selected by feature ablation: candidates were added to or removed from the model one at a time under the same cross-validation protocol, and a candidate was retained only if it lowered the out-of-fold prediction error. Ablation was preferred to a filter measure such as Information Gain because it measures directly whether a feature improves the prediction of the model being evaluated. The same procedure tested whether EVI and NDWI, which are computed from the same bands as NDVI, add information beyond NDVI.</p>
<p class="tcap"><b>Table 4</b></p><p>Data Dictionary of Input Attributes and Target Variable</p>
<table><tr><th>Item</th><th>Description</th><th>What It Looks Like</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Nature</th><th>Data Type</th><th>Source</th></tr>
<tr><td>NDVI</td><td>Normalized Difference Vegetation Index; contrasts near-infrared against red reflectance.</td><td>Monthly mean, −1 to 1; higher values indicate denser, healthier vegetation.</td><td>Indicator of crop vigor across the season.</td><td>Predictor (numeric)</td><td>float</td><td>Sentinel-2</td></tr>
<tr><td>EVI</td><td>Enhanced Vegetation Index; reduces atmospheric and soil-background influence relative to NDVI.</td><td>Monthly mean, approximately −1 to 1.</td><td>Vegetation condition where NDVI saturates in dense canopy.</td><td>Candidate predictor (numeric)</td><td>float</td><td>Sentinel-2</td></tr>
<tr><td>NDWI</td><td>Normalized Difference Water Index; contrasts green against near-infrared reflectance.</td><td>Monthly mean, −1 to 1; higher values indicate more surface water.</td><td>Field flooding and canopy moisture.</td><td>Candidate predictor (numeric)</td><td>float</td><td>Sentinel-2</td></tr>
<tr><td>VV and VH backscatter</td><td>Sentinel-1 C-band SAR backscatter in vertical-vertical and vertical-horizontal polarization, terrain-corrected.</td><td>Monthly mean in decibels (dB), typically negative.</td><td>Crop structure and surface moisture regardless of cloud cover.</td><td>Predictor (numeric)</td><td>float</td><td>Sentinel-1</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rainfall</td><td>Precipitation accumulated over the month.</td><td>Millimeters (mm).</td><td>Water availability for rice growth.</td><td>Predictor (numeric)</td><td>float</td><td>Open-Meteo API</td></tr>
<tr><td>Temperature</td><td>Mean air temperature over the month.</td><td>Degrees Celsius (°C).</td><td>Governs crop development rate and yield formation.</td><td>Predictor (numeric)</td><td>float</td><td>Open-Meteo API</td></tr>
<tr><td>Relative Humidity</td><td>Mean relative humidity over the month.</td><td>Percentage (0–100%).</td><td>Influences transpiration, disease pressure, and crop growth.</td><td>Predictor (numeric)</td><td>float</td><td>Open-Meteo API</td></tr>
<tr><td>Historical Rice Yield (target)</td><td>Recorded seasonal rice production per unit area.</td><td>Metric tons per hectare (mt/ha).</td><td>Ground-truth label for training, validation, and evaluation.</td><td>Target</td><td>float</td><td>PhilRice (municipal); City Agriculture Offices (barangay)</td></tr>
</table>
"""

FEATURE_ENG = """<h4>Feature Engineering and Data Preparation</h4>
<p>The monthly values were organized into cropping-season sequences. Following the semester convention of the Philippine Statistics Authority, the dry season was taken as January to June and the wet season as July to December, so that each municipality-season is described by six monthly timesteps. Each sequence was joined with the observed yield of the same municipality and season, producing one training record per observed yield. Within each cross-validation fold, every feature was standardized to a z-score using the mean and standard deviation of the training years only, so that no information from the withheld year entered the model; a month with a missing value, mainly an optical month fully obscured by cloud, was set to the training mean after standardization. Barangay sequences were built in the same way from the barangay feature tables. Figure 6 summarizes the preparation steps.</p>
"""

ARCH_TO_ANALYSIS = """<h4>CNN-LSTM Architecture</h4>
<p>The prediction model consists of two parts:</p>
<p>- Convolutional layers: one-dimensional convolutions slide across the six monthly timesteps and learn short-term patterns among the input features, such as a drop in vegetation index accompanied by heavy rainfall.</p>
<p>- Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) layers: the convolutional outputs are passed to two LSTM layers, which follow how conditions develop across the cropping season and relate that development to the final yield.</p>
<p>Fully connected layers then map the last LSTM state to a single predicted yield for the municipality-season.</p>
<h4>Model Training and Validation</h4>
<p>The model was evaluated with leave-one-year-out cross-validation across the eight years from 2018 to 2025. In each of the eight folds the model was trained on seven years and used to predict the withheld year, and the out-of-fold predictions were pooled so that every observation was predicted by a model that had not seen its year. This protocol was chosen instead of a random training, validation, and test split because rice yield is correlated within a year; a random split would let the model learn from other records of the same year and would overstate its accuracy. Within each fold, 15% of the training records were set aside to decide when to stop training, as described in Table 3.</p>
<p>The architecture and hyperparameters of the previous AgriKA model were retained rather than re-tuned, so that the comparison between the baseline and enhanced configurations measures the effect of Sentinel-1 and not the effect of tuning. A Random Forest regressor trained on the same features and folds was included as a reference model.</p>
<h4>Model Output</h4>
<p>The final model produced a predicted seasonal yield for each municipality-season. To obtain barangay predictions, the municipality-trained model was applied to the barangay feature sequences of the 42 barangays with reported harvests, since barangay records were too few and too short to train a separate model. The predictions were stored in the spatial database and displayed in AgriKA-GIS as predicted-yield and residual maps.</p>
<p><b>Phase 4. System Evaluation.</b> The completed system was verified through functional, compatibility, and security test cases, and then evaluated by the respondents through User Acceptance Testing. Respondents used AgriKA-GIS and rated it on the 14-item ISO/IEC 25010 questionnaire.</p>
<p><b>Phase 5. Collection and Organization of Evaluation Data.</b> The questionnaire responses were recorded through the online form, checked for completeness, and organized in a spreadsheet for statistical analysis of the acceptability of AgriKA-GIS.</p>
"""

