SYNTH_23 = """<h3 id="synthesis-3">Synthesis</h3>
<p>The reviewed studies show that the suitability of a model depends on the form of the data. Convolutional networks learn spatial and spectral patterns from imagery (Fernandez-Beltran et al., 2021; Tanaka et al., 2023), recurrent networks such as the LSTM follow how crop conditions change through a season (Li et al., 2025), and hybrid CNN-LSTM models combine the two and have performed well in multi-temporal yield prediction, including the previous AgriKA study in Laguna (Jeong et al., 2022; Lu et al., 2024; Ebron et al., 2025). Tree-based ensembles such as Random Forest and XGBoost remain strong on engineered tabular features (Choudhary et al., 2022; Sah et al., 2024), and Vision Transformers require more training data than a single-province study can provide (Inderka et al., 2024). Studies that combined Sentinel-1 radar with Sentinel-2 optical features also reported better rice monitoring and yield prediction than single-sensor approaches (Ramadhani et al., 2020; Sah et al., 2024).</p>
<p>On this basis, the present study retained the CNN-LSTM of the previous AgriKA study, so that the contribution of Sentinel-1 could be measured against an established model applied to the same province, and included a Random Forest as a reference model, given the evidence that tree-based ensembles can match or exceed deep models when training data are limited.</p>
"""

DEVIATION = """<h3>Changes from the Approved Proposal</h3>
<p>Chapter 3 describes the methods as carried out. Several of them differ from the approved proposal, and the changes are recorded here with their reasons.</p>
<p>1. Vegetation indices. The proposal named the Green Ratio among the Sentinel-2 indices. The Normalized Difference Water Index was computed in its place. As reported under Objective 1, both EVI and NDWI were found to degrade model performance and were excluded from the final feature set. The Green Ratio was not acquired and its contribution was therefore not tested; given that the two other indices computed from the same bands were redundant with NDVI on this dataset, a substantial contribution appears unlikely, but this remains an untested expectation rather than a finding.</p>
<p>2. Image selection. The proposal described selecting one representative image per growth stage for each barangay and cropping cycle. The study instead used monthly statistics per area from the Sentinel Hub Statistical API, which give one consistent value per area and month for every source and can be repeated automatically by the weekly pipeline required by the third objective.</p>
<p>3. Validation. The proposal specified a random 70/15/15 training, validation, and test split. Leave-one-year-out cross-validation was used instead, because rice yield is correlated within a year and a random split would let the model learn from other records of the withheld year.</p>
<p>4. Feature selection. The proposal specified Information Gain on discretized yield categories. Feature ablation under the same cross-validation protocol was used instead, because it measures directly whether a feature improves the prediction of the model.</p>
<p>5. Level of the model. The proposal described a barangay-level model. Barangay harvest records were available for only 42 barangays and from 2020 onward, too few to train a separate model, so the model was trained on municipality-level records and applied to the barangay features.</p>
<p>6. Respondents. The proposal defined the UAT population as 26 personnel of the four target City Agriculture Offices and the Provincial Agriculture Office, all of whom were invited. The evaluation was opened to agricultural personnel of other Laguna agriculture offices; the final respondents were 19 personnel from eight offices.</p>
<p>7. Scope of automation. The objective names historical rice yield data among the datasets managed by the pipeline. Acquisition of the yield records is not automated, because PhilRice and the City Agriculture Offices provide no programmatic interface to them. The records are uploaded by authorized personnel through the system, where they are validated, restricted to the assigned city of the account, and recorded in the import log. Automation in this study therefore applies to the satellite and meteorological sources and to every stage after acquisition.</p>
"""
